K-FOG: Role-Owned Choice and Dependent Interaction

The current manuscript is main.tex and its root-level inputs. Build it with:

    make paper

The PDF is written to build/main.pdf. The build uses latexmk, pdfLaTeX, and
BibTeX. Run the finite semantic checks and verify the original archive with:

    make check

This is a research draft, with the novelty question stated explicitly in
the introduction. The public dependent core is background from dependent
interaction and session typing. Full strategies are retained separately
from executable plans. A finite rational-outcome algebra records the winners
of pure subgame-perfect profiles. The disclosure section has a protocol
grammar, phase typing, audience-derived views, and a two-execution hiding
theorem for prefixes excluding dependency-mediated releases.

General open-network cut, a complete logical proof calculus, and control
operators are development targets. The Python checks exercise only the
finite models, not the dependent data calculus or a proof assistant.

The previous manuscript, including its bibliography and interpreter sketch,
is preserved in archive/manuscript-2026-10-28. MANIFEST.sha256 records each
original file's content hash. Independent notes/ and research/ material is
retained separately and is not an input to the current paper.

The first public-core rewrite, before responding to the external review,
is preserved in archive/public-core-draft-2026-10-28 with its own manifest.
Both archives are verified by make check.

Start the active algebraic research with research/minimal/general.tex and
symmetric.tex for owned choice, and reset.tex for its smallest classified
binary fragment. The intended full signature includes internal
role-switching. Reset retains a resolved right operand and otherwise
unions. Its complete all-arity semantics is an antimatroid with one
initial element; canonical prerequisite antichains give polynomial
equality, realizability, counterexamples, and synthesis. Every equation
is determined by a three-element chain, the dual of Waldhauser's known
G_3, and exactly the order algebra O_(2+1) of Freese et al. (2002).
Their Theorem 3.7 supplies a complete six-equation reset basis, reproduced
in reset.tex. Related path normal forms and forest semantics are also
known; the prerequisite/antimatroid presentation needs comparison with
those forms before claiming novelty. The intrinsic connection to owned
choice is a candidate contribution with priority still open. Reset is a proper
fragment and has the same equational theory for every K>=3. Mixed-input
semantics, three-element completeness, and compilation to owned-choice
terms and soundness of all six basis equations are proved in Lean;
basis completeness is imported from the published theorem. The
antimatroid results have written proofs
and independent finite checks.
Reset also has an intrinsic characterization: the binary idempotent
atom-preserving definable operations are exactly the two projections
and the two orientations of reset. Their generated clone is the reset
calculus. Ternary atom-preserving operations already retain additional
owner-sensitive behavior. A one-constant rigidity lemma supporting the
binary classification is kernel-checked in OneConstant.lean; the full
classification uses the earlier mathematical atomic-projection result.

Its conservative models are exactly forest orders: comparable arguments
return the higher one and incomparable arguments return the right one.
Two equations characterize them within the conservative class. Comb
forests give subdirectly irreducible models of every infinite cardinality,
giving explicit witnesses for the already known residual largeness of
this variety. Order reconstruction and the generic comb-monolith argument
are checked in ResetModels.lean; membership of forest models in the
reset variety uses a written closure proof. This does not imply the
corresponding residual result for the larger owned-choice signature.

For the full language, the main simplification is intrinsic: every
idempotent definable operation already uses choices and variables alone.
Its player-view decomposition
survives, and idempotency needs optimally logarithmically many fixed tests.
The deterministic atomic fragment at every arity is exactly the known
clone S_2 (essentially unary or at most two-valued functions). A common-leaf
separation theorem explains the constraint and is proved in Lean with
internal switches explicit. Relative reset connects atomic and ambiguous
behavior; two-permutation ambiguity actions have exact cyclewise fibers.
Earlier results include optimal singleton/pair tests at every arity;
the exact general-K ambiguity-action monoid, with canonical rows and a
perfect-matching synthesis criterion; coNP-complete equational validity
already at fixed K=3 without constants or switches; and the exact three-role unary
classification (751 functions). The general monoid explicitly reuses the
known factorpower of the symmetric group, with constants and an ambiguity
quotient. structure.tex analyzes the reduct and congruences; report.tex
supplies the initial unary results. Run make check-minimal for Lean and
finite checks, and make paper-minimal for all five PDFs in build/minimal.
The directory README distinguishes generic proofs from mathematical
arguments and finite experiments. The full owned-choice signature and
its idempotent core still lack an equational axiomatization here; reset
has the known finite basis. Polynomial equality for reset cannot extend
to the whole idempotent core unless P=NP. Priority of the owned-choice
connections remains open. The dependent manuscript is a companion
foundation: no general theorem yet connects dependent cut, disclosure,
and rational-outcome composition.
